Every test follows the same arc. The test statistic measures how far the
estimate sits from the null value, in standard-error units:\text{test statistic} = \frac{\text{point estimate} - \text{null value}}
{\text{standard error (under } H_0)}

A large statistic produces a small p-value = P(\text{result at least this
extreme} \mid H_0 \text{ true}). Reject H_0 when p-value < \alpha (the
significance level).

One proportion (H_0: p = p_0): the SE uses the null value p_0.z = \frac{\hat{p} - p_0}{\sqrt{\dfrac{p_0(1-p_0)}{n}}}

Two proportions (H_0: p_1 = p_2): use the pooled proportion
\hat{p} = \dfrac{x_1 + x_2}{n_1 + n_2} (total successes over total sample) for
the test SE:z = \frac{\hat{p}_1 - \hat{p}_2}
{\sqrt{\hat{p}(1-\hat{p})\left(\dfrac{1}{n_1} + \dfrac{1}{n_2}\right)}}

where x_1, x_2 = successes and n_1, n_2 = sample sizes in each group.

One mean (H_0: \mu = \mu_0), df = n-1:t = \frac{\bar{x} - \mu_0}{s/\sqrt{n}}

Paired mean — reduce the pairs to differences d_i, then run a one-sample
t on the differences (\bar{x}_d = mean difference, s_d = SD of
differences, df = n-1):t = \frac{\bar{x}_d - 0}{s_d/\sqrt{n}}

Two independent means (H_0: \mu_1 = \mu_2):t = \frac{(\bar{x}_1 - \bar{x}_2) - 0}
{\sqrt{\dfrac{s_1^2}{n_1} + \dfrac{s_2^2}{n_2}}}

with degrees of freedom from software (Welch’s approximation; a conservative
hand value is df = \min(n_1 - 1,\ n_2 - 1)).
